<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip II, Duke of Pomerania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1573">1573</a>–<a href="/years/1618">1618</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Philip II of Pomerania-Stettin · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">House of Griffins</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip II was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-stettin">Pomerania-Stettin</a> from 1606 to 1618, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffin dynasty</a>. A learned collector and patron of the arts, he commissioned the Pomeranian Art Cabinet through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> agent Philipp Hainhofer and the great map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eilhard-lubinus">Eilhard Lubinus</a>. He died childless, and the Griffin line ended with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a> in 1637.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-duke-of-pomerania.jpg" alt="Philip II, Duke of Pomerania" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unbekannter Künstler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhilippII.1678.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was born on 29 July 1573 at Neuenkamp, later Franzburg, the eldest son of Duke Bogislaw XIII of Pomerania-Barth and his first wife, Clara of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, and he grew up at his father's small residence in Barth. His interests soon went beyond the usual education of a German prince of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>: at twelve he owned a collection of books and pictures, and at 17 he wrote his first scientific papers. At 18 he wrote that he collected good books, portraits from a master's hand and ancient coins to improve himself and to benefit the public. His last grand tour ended with two years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, cut short in 1598 when his mother fell seriously ill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Duke Barnim X died in 1603, Bogislaw XIII became ruling duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-stettin">Pomerania-Stettin</a> but, apparently feeling too old to govern, appointed Philip its governor; on his father's death in 1606 Philip ruled in his own right. Besides his patronage of the arts, his reign is marked by the rural regulations of 1616, which created a legal basis for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. On 10 March 1607 he married Sophia, daughter of John II, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg. Suffering from gout from the first decade of the 17th century, he withdrew more and more from public life, missing his brother Francis's wedding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> in 1610 and the Diet of Regensburg in 1613.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Philip died on 3 February 1618, before the age of 50, like most male members of the last generation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">House of Griffins</a>. His commissions included an epitaph on the tomb of Duke Barnim VI at Kenz-Küstrow, a book of portraits of the Griffins, an art gallery and the Meier Court, and the diary Hainhofer kept on his visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1617 describes the collection at the Ducal Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>.</p><p>Many works were unfinished at Philip's death. His brother and successor, Francis, paid for their completion, among them a new wing of the castle on Mint Square that housed most of the art, but then the era of art at the Pomeranian court came to an end. The art cabinet was destroyed by fire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip II of Pomerania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Franzburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.19,12.88&amp;year=1602" aria-label="Franzburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Philip II, Duke of Pomerania"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5639 5434h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5639 5434h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.19,12.88&amp;year=1602">What was here in 1602? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
